WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:09.183
Once upon a time, there lived a woodcutter.

2
00:00:10.684 --> 00:00:12.584
Every day he went to work in the forest.

3
00:00:13.745 --> 00:00:17.386
One day, after working hard, the woodcutter decided to head home.

4
00:00:18.487 --> 00:00:21.208
He picked up his old wooden ax and began walking.

5
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.250
Soon he heard the sound of rushing water.

6
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.632
"That's strange," he thought.

7
00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:34.050
"I haven't heard that sound here before."

8
00:00:35.791 --> 00:00:38.653
Feeling thirsty, the woodcutter followed the sound.

9
00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:43.156
Through the trees he saw a waterfall.

10
00:00:44.417 --> 00:00:48.119
It was falling into a deep pond with clear, sparkling water.

11
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.061
The woodcutter stepped toward the pond.

12
00:00:51.081 --> 00:00:54.263
"Amazing," he thought.

13
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:57.105
"I never knew this pond was here."

14
00:00:58.686 --> 00:01:00.748
The woodcutter crouched to take a drink.

15
00:01:02.008 --> 00:01:08.291
Suddenly—splash! He saw several ugly green frogs hop out of the pond.

16
00:01:09.811 --> 00:01:11.452
Then he noticed something else.

17
00:01:12.953 --> 00:01:14.213
His ax was missing!

18
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:16.114
"Oh no!"

19
00:01:17.094 --> 00:01:18.355
He looked around frantically.

20
00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:21.796
"That's my only ax!

21
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:24.477
I need it for work!"

22
00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:27.538
His eyes landed on the pond.

23
00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:31.020
"I must have dropped it in the water.

24
00:01:32.652 --> 00:01:34.493
That was what made the splash."

25
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:39.834
The woodcutter slumped down, holding his head in his hands.

26
00:01:41.014 --> 00:01:42.195
He didn't know how to swim.

27
00:01:43.275 --> 00:01:45.836
And he didn't have enough money to buy a new ax.

28
00:01:47.356 --> 00:01:50.397
"How will I work?"

29
00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:51.777
he moaned.

30
00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:55.238
"Excuse me," a voice said.

31
00:01:55.258 --> 00:01:58.679
"Something fell into my pond."

32
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.614
In front of him, a fairy was holding a glimmering ax.

33
00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:08.097
"Is this silver ax yours?"

34
00:02:08.597 --> 00:02:09.398
the fairy asked.

35
00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:13.861
"No," the woodcutter said.

36
00:02:13.881 --> 00:02:18.844
"My ax fell into the pond, but that one isn't mine."

37
00:02:21.646 --> 00:02:25.289
The fairy slipped silently into the water without making a ripple.

38
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.976
She quickly returned with another magnificent ax.

39
00:02:32.036 --> 00:02:33.517
"Is this gold ax yours?"

40
00:02:34.078 --> 00:02:34.658
she asked.

41
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.040
The woodcutter shook his head.

42
00:02:38.061 --> 00:02:43.885
"No, my ax was a simple old ax made of wood."

43
00:02:43.905 --> 00:02:48.168
"I see," the fairy said.

44
00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:52.471
She disappeared into the water again, and reappeared.

45
00:02:52.491 --> 00:02:56.673
"Is this wooden ax yours?"

46
00:02:58.435 --> 00:02:59.976
The woodcutter's face lit up.

47
00:03:01.412 --> 00:03:02.853
"Yes, that's it!

48
00:03:04.074 --> 00:03:05.616
May I have it back, please?"

49
00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:10.019
The fairy handed the ax to the woodcutter.

50
00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:13.122
"You are a very honest man.

51
00:03:13.142 --> 00:03:16.564
I would like to reward your honesty.

52
00:03:18.026 --> 00:03:20.528
I will give you the silver and gold axes."

53
00:03:20.548 --> 00:03:27.693
"Thank you," the woodcutter said, delighted with his good fortune.

54
00:03:30.255 --> 00:03:33.237
At home, the woodcutter told his neighbor what had happened.

55
00:03:38.278 --> 00:03:39.038
the neighbor said.

56
00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:43.920
The neighbor grabbed a wooden ax and ran into the forest.

57
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.181
He found the mysterious pond and hurled his ax into the water.

58
00:03:50.902 --> 00:03:52.342
For a while nothing happened.

59
00:03:53.643 --> 00:03:57.704
Meanwhile some ugly green frogs sat around the pond, staring at him.

60
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.742
"What are you looking at?"

61
00:04:03.162 --> 00:04:03.823
the man said.

62
00:04:05.504 --> 00:04:10.748
Suddenly the fairy appeared out of nowhere, holding a magnificent silver ax.

63
00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:13.811
"Is this your silver ax?"

64
00:04:14.311 --> 00:04:15.152
the fairy asked.

65
00:04:15.172 --> 00:04:20.256
"Yes!" The man nodded eagerly.

66
00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:25.760
"I guess I dropped it—" "Lies!"

67
00:04:26.281 --> 00:04:27.221
the fairy snapped.

68
00:04:27.241 --> 00:04:31.857
"This is not your silver ax, and you did not drop it.

69
00:04:33.957 --> 00:04:36.799
You are a liar, and I don't like liars.

70
00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:40.720
Now I will punish you!"

71
00:04:42.441 --> 00:04:43.861
The man shook with fear.

72
00:04:43.881 --> 00:04:46.402
"I'm sorry.

73
00:04:46.422 --> 00:04:47.963
Please don't—" Zap!

74
00:04:50.124 --> 00:04:53.445
An ugly green frog now sat in the man's place.

75
00:04:55.186 --> 00:04:56.507
The fairy smiled.

76
00:04:56.527 --> 00:04:59.628
"Nothing good comes from lying.

77
00:05:01.409 --> 00:05:04.490
Honesty is always the best policy!"
